{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>If the men have come to call you, etc.</b> One needs to know why He spoke His words in language of doubt — is anything beyond Him? Further, why did HaShem agree for the wicked man to go to curse the enemies of Israel? Further, one needs to know why HaShem retracted from His initial position, in which He restrained him — at first what was His reasoning, and ultimately what was His reasoning?" "It appears that in the matter before us there were two paths before HaShem, and in each of them there was an improper concern. One: if HaShem were to consent to his going, those nations would think that he was under his own authority and they would not know that the dog's chain is in its master's hand and he cannot do whatever he pleases — and HaShem wanted it to be made known before the eyes of the nations that Balaam is not at liberty to harm one whom HaShem does not wish harmed. And if He were not to let him go, another concern would enter, for Balaam would say that He is afraid of him, heaven forbid, to send him, and for this reason He prevents him from going. Therefore HaShem devised a strategy and removed both concerns in the proper way. At first He said to him <b>you shall not go</b>, etc., by which He showed the nations that the evil dog is not under his own authority. He also devised a strategy here to pay him his reward, for he had anticipated by saying to Balak's emissaries that he is not under his own authority. And what reward did He give him — that HaShem knew what this journey would call upon him: that besides the fact that it would bring him no benefit, as it is written (Numbers 24:11) ״אָמַרְתִּי כַּבֵּד אֲכַבֶּדְךָ וְהִנֵּה מְנָעֲךָ ה׳ מִכָּבוֹד״ — besides this, this journey would further cause him to fall by the sword, as it is written (Numbers 31:8) ״וְאֶת בִּלְעָם וְגוֹ׳ הָרְגוּ בֶּחָרֶב״, when he went to receive his reward for the counsel he gave them during his journey. Therefore HaShem restrained him from descending into the pit in payment for his words, and said to him ״לֹא תֵלֵךְ עִמָּהֶם״, for the Holy One, blessed be He, does not withhold the reward of any creature. And this is in addition to the measure of honor He showed him by upgrading the emissaries, as we wrote above." "<b>And with the second embassy</b> that Balak sent, we come to rectify the second concern — that they might say that HaShem cannot, heaven forbid, prevent him from harming Israel through his going, and this error would enter Balaam's heart and he would think thoughts, that HaShem would not have dominion over him there to prevent him from doing his will, for he had already been tested and found to be a privy of filth (מֵי רַגְלַיִם). Therefore He said to him <b>arise, go with them</b>, and in every place My hands hold sway over you. And He did not say to him outright ״arise, go,״ but in this manner: <b>if to call you</b>, etc. — meaning, if the call of the men holds any pleasure for you. Here He hinted to him that there is no benefit for him in this journey, and He made the thing conditional upon whether this journey was for his good, so that he would exert himself in his sorceries to know its outcome, and from this he would know what would befall him and would desist. And the intent of the statement ״if to call״ is not a doubt before Him but rather a condition of the matter — if there is benefit for him in it, etc. And through this the concern was removed that Balaam might say that HaShem was afraid, heaven forbid, of him to send him — for after all, He sent him. He also hinted to him that perhaps he would sit and not pursue what would be pursued from the harm to Israel. Also in this He paid him his reward as we have written, and restrained him from falling by the sword if his soul would not desire evil — but his soul is the soul of a wicked man, and he paid no heed to what HaShem had aroused in him. Or perhaps it is for this that His anger burned when HaShem saw him going without heeding what He had aroused in him — this would prove to him with certainty that there was benefit for him, and this could only be if he thought that the matter for which He sent him would succeed in his hand. Therefore the anger of the Elohim of Israel burned against him, and through this all the fine points are resolved. And nearby, an additional reason for ״וַיִּחַר אַף״, etc., will be resolved."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:12:49.805079625Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}